ore acting, acts on a guess, re-asks\n52\t  after consent, miscomputes a total) — adding ANOTHER prose rule it will skip the same\n53\t  way does nothing. Enforce it in CODE: an in-body guard / validation / computation in\n54\t  the EXISTING tool, or a composite tool that performs the whole action. Prose is reserved\n55\t  for genuine KNOWLEDGE gaps (a fact/format/criterion the agent cannot derive).\n56\t- **Do NOT defer a capability-gap cluster** (\"would need a compute tool — next iteration\").\n57\t  If a cluster needs a compute/validation/composite tool, BUILD it THIS iteration — that\n58\t  is the highest-leverage edit available, not something to postpone.\n59\tSo for each cluster, first ask \"can this be enforced in `tools.py` code?\" — if yes, do\n60\tthat; only fall back to a prompt rule for a true knowledge gap. Expect your `tools.py`\n61\tdiff to contain real CODE (guards, validations, new/changed tool bodies), not only\n62\tdocstring text — across SEVERAL tools, not one.\n63\t\n64\t## The THREE TESTS every change must pass (this is the whole game)\n65\tBefore you keep any edit, confirm all three. Drop any edit that fails even one.\n66\t1. **REAL** — it targets a cluster that is FAILING in THIS iteration's `./trajectories/`\n67\t   (reward 0, partial-credit, or communication/omission). Never edit for a hypothetical\n68\t   problem, never touch a path only used by already-PASSING tasks.\n69\t2. **SAFE (bounded blast radius)** — the real regression question is *behavioral*:\n70\t   **would this edit change what the agent DOES on ANY currently-passing task?** Not\n71\t   \"does a passing task call this tool\" — \"does the agent now take a different action,\n72\t   or newly ACT where it correctly REFUSED / escalated\". Two blast-radius classes:\n73\t   - **BOUNDED** — an in-body guard/computation that fires ONLY on the exact violating\n74\t     condition. Only already-failing inputs hit it; passing tasks are untouched by\n75\t     construction. This is the SAFE default — prefer it.\n76\t   - **UNBOUNDED** — any edit to a GLOBAL decision/permission/refusal rule in the prompt\n77\t     (loosening \"X may do Y\", broadening who may take an action, relaxing a\n78\t     refuse-and-escalate rule). It changes behavior across the ENTIRE decision class,\n79\t     including tasks where the stricter/original behavior was the gold answer. Allowed\n80\t     ONLY if the new behavior is correct for EVERY task in that class AND you have read\n81\t     the currently-passing tasks in the class and confirmed none relied on the old\n82\t     behavior. Otherwise it is a guaranteed regression — encode the discriminating\n83\t     CONDITION instead (see the DECISION / PERMISSION lever below).\n84\t   Name the passing tasks in each edit's blast-radius class and state which class it is.\n85\t   A regression wastes the whole candidate (the gate rejects a net-zero), not one task.\n86\t3. **VERIFIED** — you have shown it actually fixes its target (see VERIFY-THE-FIX). An\n87\t   edit you cannot verify is a guess — drop it.\n88\t\n89\tQuality AND breadth: ship every fix that passes the three tests — the more clusters you\n90\tcover safely, the bigger the gain. The only edits to leave out are the speculative ones\n91\t(an edit that fails a test), not real fixes you ran out of patience for. Don't re-add\n92\tanything `LEDGER.md` / `JOURNAL.md` show was already tried and rejected.\n93\t\n94\t## Read these first (everything is in this working directory)\n95\t- **`./guidance/<cap>/SKILL.md` for EACH selected capability — READ IT IN FULL before you\n96\t  edit; it is the MENU of improvement TYPES available to you.** Each capability skill lists\n97\t  the concrete kinds of change you can make (for a tools capability: in-body validation\n98\t  guards, normalization, computation, composite/atomic-write tools, loop/workflow tools,\n99\t  enriched return values + actionable errors, add/remove tools, sharpened descriptions;\n100\t  for a prompt capability: role/contract, decision-rule narrowing, missing-rule, worked\n101\t  example, consolidation). Do not invent change types from memory — take them from the\n102\t  skill, and deliberately apply MULTIPLE DIFFERENT types this iteration (e.g. several\n103\t  in-body guards + a compute tool + enriched errors + a couple of prompt rules), matching\n104\t  each cluster to the strongest type the skill describes for it. (Tool code? also read\n105\t  `./guidance/sources/` for the data models/types so your code is correct.)\n106\t- `./guidance/diagnose/SKILL.md` — the failure-clustering method. Use it.\n107\t- `./trajectories/` — the FULL traces of the current best candidate (the step you build\n108\t  on). The `## (b) 1 FLAKY task(s) — pass sometimes; make the good behavior consistent (full traces in ./trajectories/):\n109\t(reward is the mean over trials — the honest signal; the feedback line is from the LAST trial and may say 'passed' even when the mean is below 1.)\n110\t- case-019-content-vs-code-split (reward 0.75): decomposition_quality 4.0/5 — SCORE: 4\n111\t\n112\t**HLR Coverage — solid.** All four HLRs map to epics with per-epic traceability sections and a complete matrix: HLR-1→E002, HLR-2→E003, HLR-3→E001, HLR-4→E004; nothing orphaned. Priority inheritance is handled correctly and explicitly justified: E001 (nominally P1 via HLR-3) is raised to P0 because it gates both P0 epics. E004 is docs-authoring and terminal \n113\t` block below summarizes them with argument-level feedback — read\n114\t  the actual traces for the clusters you'll fix, don't rely on the summary alone.\n115\t- `./LEDGER.md` — FACTS (read-only): every prior iteration's outcome + the exact tasks it\n116\t  broke/fixed. Your SAFE test starts here — never re-introduce a change that broke a task.\n117\t- `./JOURNAL.md` — the accumulating handover. Each entry is the optimizer's INTENT, and\n118\t  directly below it the framework stamps a **RESULT** line (objective: ACCEPTED/REJECTED ·\n119\t  Δ · the exact tasks fixed/broke). The RESULT lines — not the intent — are the truth of\n120\t  what worked: read them all before proposing. If the most recent RESULT is **REJECTED**,\n121\t  its batch was reverted; read that entry's `./prior_iterations/<id>/diff.patch`, keep the\n122\t  edits that did NOT appear in its `broke={...}`, and DROP or REDESIGN the ones that did —\n123\t  do NOT resubmit the whole rejected batch, and do NOT abandon the cluster. APPEND your new\n124\t  entry (intent only) below the marker; never edit earlier entries or re-try a refuted idea.\n125\t- `./RUNMAP.md` + `./prior_iterations/<id>/` — EVERY prior iteration's (accepted AND\n126\t  rejected) PROCESS.md + diff.patch. Read the one(s) that touched a cluster you're about to\n127\t  work on, so you build on what worked and avoid repeating what regressed.\n128\t- `./PROCESS.md` — your REQUIRED explainability file for THIS iteration (template inside).\n129\t- `./guidance/optimizer/<name>.md` — your agent's subagent/parallelism features (optional).\n130\t\n131\t\n132\t## Process (do this, then STOP)\n133\t**Parallelism:** Your agent supports parallel subagents/worktrees (see ./guidance/optimizer/claude-code.md). FAN OUT to cover MANY clusters at once: one read-only subagent per trajectory-group to diagnose, then one edit-subagent per issue (each in its own worktree), then MERGE every edit into this ONE candidate with no conflicts. This is how a single iteration fixes many issues across many trajectories, not just the biggest one.\n134\t1. Read your capability SKILL(s) + the diagnose method + the cross-iteration files\n135\t   (LEDGER facts, JOURNAL handover, RUNMAP for clusters you'll touch).\n136\t2. Diagnose THIS iteration's `./trajectories/` ONLY (not stale signatures). Cluster ALL\n137\t   failures by shared root cause — total, partial-credit, AND communication/omission.\n138\t   RANK clusters by LEVERAGE = (# failing tasks × trials × score recoverable), biggest\n139\t   first — but plan to fix ALL of them this iteration, not only the top few.\n140\t3. For EACH cluster, pick the strongest improvement TYPE from the capability SKILL(s)\n141\t   (cross-check the FAILURE TYPE section next) and draft the edit. Across the iteration\n142\t   use MULTIPLE different types from the skills, not the same one repeatedly. Run each\n143\t   edit through the THREE TESTS; keep it only if it passes all three.\n144\t4. Ship every passing edit together in this ONE candidate — cover as many clusters as you\n145\t   can SAFELY (that is the win), and never include an edit that fails a test.\n146\t5. Fill `PROCESS.md` and APPEND your entry to `JOURNAL.md`. STOP.\n147\t\n148\t## Choose the lever by FAILURE TYPE\n149\tPick the strongest lever YOUR capability's edit space offers (see `./guidance/<cap>/`).\n150\tThe levers below are written for a TOOLS capability; for a prompt-only capability\n151\t(system-prompt / skill-package) use the structural-prose equivalent noted in each.\n152\t\n153\t- **RULE VIOLATION** — the agent breaks a rule/precondition/formula it could already\n154\t  follow. **Default strong lever (tools): move the rule INTO THE CODE BODY of the\n155\t  EXISTING tool that governs it** — an in-body validation / normalization / computation\n156\t  that raises an ACTIONABLE error or returns the corrected value, scoped to fire ONLY on\n157\t  the violating condition. This is the highest-yield, lowest-regression edit and is what\n158\t  drove the best prior results — reach for it first. *Example:*\n159\t  ```\n160\t  def book(record_id, amount, payment_id):\n161\t  +   methods = {m[\"id\"] for m in get_record(record_id)[\"payment_methods\"]}\n162\t  +   if payment_id not in methods:                      # fires only on the violation\n163\t  +       raise ValueError(f\"payment_id {payment_id!r} not on file; available={sorted(methods)}\")\n164\t      return _backend.book(record_id, amount, payment_id)\n165\t  ```\n166\t  (Prompt capability: make the rule unmissable — a checklisted step / worked counterexample.)\n167\t- **CAPABILITY GAP / ACTION STALL** — the agent has NO reliable way to do the thing (a\n168\t  hard-ZERO cluster needing a real compute / composite / discriminating-predicate tool),\n169\t  or it narrates/confirms a multi-step action then never executes it. **Prose does\n170\t  NOTHING for a hard zero** — a 0.00 task stays 0.00 after any docstring/prompt reword;\n171\t  it needs a TOOL the agent will CALL that changes the graded state. (tools) ADD a NEW\n172\t  code-bearing tool that closes the gap — a composite atomic-WRITE tool whose body\n173\t  performs the whole action via the existing primitives (then REMOVE the raw primitives\n174\t  so it can't be skipped), or a loop/validation tool. Add a new tool ONLY when it closes\n175\t  a real gap AND the agent will call it AND it changes the graded outcome — NOT a\n176\t  read/compute/summary helper that a guard or a prompt line would subsume, and never to\n177\t  hit a quota. (Prompt capability: an explicit, ordered, unavoidable procedure.)\n178\t- **KNOWLEDGE GAP** — a format/criterion/fact the agent genuinely cannot derive →\n179\t  prose: a precise prompt or docstring rule. Don't restate a rule the agent already has;\n180\t  that's a rule-violation (use code), not a knowledge gap.\n181\t- **DECISION / PERMISSION (ACT vs REFUSE)** — the agent made the wrong call on a\n182\t  decision the policy governs: it ACTED where it should have refused/escalated, or\n183\t  refused where it should have acted. **This is the most dangerous cluster to fix\n184\t  wrong.** NEVER loosen, broaden, or alter a GLOBAL decision/permission/refusal rule in\n185\t  the prompt to fix it — a global prose change (e.g. \"restricted records MAY now be\n186\t  modified\") flips behavior for the WHOLE class and regresses every currently-passing task where\n187\t  the original, stricter behavior was the gold answer (this exact mistake sank a prior\n188\t  run). Instead encode the EXACT discriminating CONDITION that separates the qualifying\n189\t  cases, **ideally in CODE** — an in-body guard on the tool that owns the action, which\n190\t  refuses/raises ONLY when the precise policy predicate is/isn't met (bounded blast\n191\t  radius: only the qualifying cases change). If it truly cannot be code, add an ADDITIVE\n192\t  prompt rule that NARROWS (states the exact predicate) — never one that LOOSENS.\n193\t\n194\tAlso improve **tool RETURN values / error messages** (actionable: what's wrong + valid\n195\toptions + next step) when a recoverable error stranded the agent — this is high-leverage\n196\tand low-risk. Never delete a needed rule; change/consolidate instead.\n197\t\n198\t## VERIFY-THE-FIX (do this for EACH kept edit — it satisfies the VERIFIED + SAFE tests)\n199\t- **In-body guard / computation:** run the tool body on the EXACT args from the failing\n200\t  trace and confirm it fires / returns the corrected value. THEN run it on the args from\n201\t  1–2 currently-PASSING tasks that use the same tool and confirm it does NOT fire (the\n202\t  blast-radius / SAFE check). A guard that never fires on the failing task is dead code;\n203\t  one that fires on a passing task is a regression — drop or rescope either.\n204\t- **Decision / permission edit (prompt OR guard):** the SAFE check is BEHAVIORAL, not\n205\t  argument-level. Enumerate the currently-passing tasks in the SAME decision class (same\n206\t  permission/refusal rule) and confirm the edit would NOT flip the agent's action on any\n207\t  of them — in particular that it does not make the agent newly ACT where a passing\n208\t  task's gold answer was to refuse/escalate. If you cannot enumerate and check that\n209\t  class, the edit is UNBOUNDED and unverified — replace it with a coded\n210\t  discriminating-condition guard scoped to the violating cases only.\n211\t- **New tool:** construct the inputs the agent SHOULD have passed (from the trace's\n212\t  observed state) and run the tool body; confirm it completes the action end-to-end.\n213\t- **Prompt/docstring:** confirm the missing fact is now stated, general, and unambiguous.\n214\t\n215\tRecord one line per edit in PROCESS.md, e.g.\n216\t`trace <task> arg x=<bad> → guard raises \"<msg>\"; passing tasks <ids> → guard does NOT fire`.\n217\tAn edit with no verification line is unverified — verify it or drop it.\n218\t\n219\t## NON-OVERFITTING (every edit must GENERALIZE)\n220\tEvery edit encodes a GENERAL rule that holds across the whole class of inputs — NEVER a\n221\tliteral that special-cases one task (its id, target, name, or expected answer). A guard\n222\tfires on the general condition (\"the id is not in the user's records\", \"the record is in\n223\ta state that forbids this action\"), NOT `if record_id == \"<TASK_SPECIFIC_ID>\"`. ALLOWED: constants the policy/domain\n224\tdefines (the policy's stated current date, a fixed fee/threshold, a domain enum). Use\n225\tper-task specifics and any ground-truth in the traces ONLY to understand the failure\n226\tCLASS, then write the general fix.\n227\t\n228\t## Handover (REQUIRED before you STOP)\n229\t- **PROCESS.md** (this iteration): the ranked cluster list (with leverage + RULE/GAP/\n230\t  KNOWLEDGE tag), every kept edit + its lever, the VERIFY-THE-FIX + blast-radius line per\n231\t  edit, what you deliberately skipped and why, and (if you used subagents) that you did.\n232\t- **JOURNAL.md** (append ONE entry below the marker; never edit earlier entries). Write\n233\t  INTENT only — you cannot know your gate result; the framework stamps the RESULT below\n234\t  your entry: the changes I made (1 line/edit, naming the file/tool + cluster) · the\n235\t  EXPECTED effect + why each is safe · which prior RESULTS I built on and which regressing\n236\t  edits I did NOT re-try (cite ids) · refuted hypotheses (a prior RESULT disproved — never\n237\t  re-test) · high-value clusters not yet cracked + designs already tried · plateau signal +\n238\t  which lever to switch to · focus next iteration.\n239\t\n240\t## (b) 1 FLAKY task(s) — pass sometimes; make the good behavior consistent (full traces in ./trajectories/):\n241\t(reward is the mean over trials — the honest signal; the feedback line is from the LAST trial and may say 'passed' even when the mean is below 1.)\n242\t- case-019-content-vs-code-split (reward 0.75): decomposition_quality 4.0/5 — SCORE: 4\n243\t\n244\t**HLR Coverage — solid.** All four HLRs map to epics with per-epic traceability sections and a complete matrix: HLR-1→E002, HLR-2→E003, HLR-3→E001, HLR-4→E004; nothing orphaned. Priority inheritance is handled correctly and explicitly justified: E001 (nominally P1 via HLR-3) is raised to P0 because it gates both P0 epics. E004 is docs-authoring and terminal \n245\t\n246\t## Currently PASSING (7 task(s)) — your edit MUST NOT regress these\n247\tThese tasks already score ~1.0. Preserve their behavior: any edit that changes their trajectory is a regression and will be rejected. Protect: case-013-human-process-gates, case-014-tech-preview-foundation, case-015-redundant-hlrs, case-016-component-canonicalization, case-017-near-threshold-no-triage, case-018-cross-strategy-dependency, case-020-hardware-dependent-investigation\n248\t\n249\t## What you are editing (the allowed edit space)\n250\tThe capability under optimization is composed of these editable artifact(s). Use the FULL edit space below — do not limit yourself to trivial wording tweaks.\n251\t- **skill-package** — Optimize a WHOLE Agent Skill package — SKILL.md, references, and bundled scripts — under the skill-creator authoring rules.\n252\t  - Allowed edits: Edit ANY part of the package: the SKILL.md frontmatter (the `description` decides whether the skill fires at all — the highest-leverage text), the body, `references/*.md`, and the bundled `scripts/`. HIGHEST-LEVERAGE EDIT: TURN A SKIPPED PROSE STEP INTO A BUNDLED SCRIPT the body invokes by command line — a step the agent runs can't be 'forgotten' the way a body rule can, and a script's source never enters the agent's context (only its output), so it is cheaper than the prose it replaces. Two patterns to prefer: (1) the traces show the agent re-deriving the same helper or doing a deterministic transform by hand — write it once into scripts/ and say EXECUTE it, don't read it; (2) a check the agent keeps skipping — make it a script that exits non-zero. The script must be real working code (never '...' or docstring-only) with a `--self-check` entry point, because validation RUNS it before any rollout is paid for. Keep the body lean (<=500 lines) and push depth into one-level references with explicit pointers; the frontmatter/body/reference/script split is validated, so an invalid package is not scored at all.\n253\t  - Full guidance (read it): ./guidance/skill-package/SKILL.md\n254\t## How your edit is judged\n255\tAlgorithm: hill-climb:all. Your edited candidate is re-scored on the SAME held-out val tasks and compared to the current best (val reward 0.969). It is ACCEPTED only if the per-task improvement clears a significance bar (a noise margin), so a tiny or lucky change is rejected. Aim for a real, generalizing gain across a CLASS of failures — not a one-off patch to a single task (that overfits and gets rejected or hurts the held-out test).\n256\t\n257\t## Self-check before STOP\n258\t- You made your FIRST file edit well before half your turn budget ran out (a prior\n259\t  iteration was discarded for investigating past its cap with zero edits shipped).\n260\t  If you spent most of your turns reading traces, you did it wrong.\n261\t- You did NOT re-run benchmark cases through the adapter to verify — verification was\n262\t  static (trace + args + gold), and the harness's re-score is the real test.\n263\t- Every kept edit passes the THREE TESTS (REAL, SAFE, VERIFIED) and has its\n264\t  verify + blast-radius line in PROCESS.md. Drop any that doesn't.\n265\t- You read each selected capability's `./guidance/<cap>/SKILL.md` and applied MULTIPLE\n266\t  DIFFERENT improvement types it describes (not the same lever repeated).\n267\t- You addressed EVERY failing cluster you found this iteration (not just the top few),\n268\t  and did NOT defer a capability-gap cluster to \"next iteration\" — you built the tool now.\n269\t- Your `tools.py` diff contains real CODE (in-body guards / validations / new or changed\n270\t  tool bodies / a composite tool) across SEVERAL tools — NOT just docstring text. If the\n271\t  only tools.py change is documentation, you under-used the iteration: convert the\n272\t  behavioral / rule-violation / capability-gap clusters into code and try again.\n273\t- Every BEHAVIORAL cluster (agent knows the rule but skips the action) is fixed in CODE,\n274\t  not by adding another prose rule it will skip the same way.\n275\t- You shipped many edits across BOTH policy.md AND tools.py — and you did NOT pad with\n276\t  speculative/cosmetic edits or re-add anything already rejected (check LEDGER/JOURNAL).\n277\t- For RULE-VIOLATION clusters on a tools capability, you used in-body guards on the\n278\t  existing tools (the default strong lever), not loose prose.\n279\t- For DECISION / PERMISSION clusters you did NOT loosen or alter a global decision/\n280\t  permission/refusal rule; you encoded the discriminating CONDITION (in code where\n281\t  possible) and confirmed it does not flip the action on any passing task in the class.\n282\t- Every prompt edit is ADDITIVE knowledge the agent lacked (a fact/format/narrowing\n283\t  predicate) — never a change to a decision the agent currently gets right.\n284\t- For any hard-ZERO cluster you shipped a real tool the agent will call (prose does\n285\t  nothing for a zero).\n286\t- Any new tool closes a real gap, will be called, and changes the graded outcome — not a\n287\t  helper a guard subsumes, not quota-filling.\n288\t- No edit hardcodes a task-specific id/value/date/answer.\n289\t- PROCESS.md + JOURNAL.md are filled. Keep narration minimal; don't restate these\n290\t  instructions or explore unrelated files.\n291\t\n292\t\n293\t## Run budget — THIS MAY BE YOUR LAST ITERATION (iteration 3/5 (2 after this one); $187.43/$5000.00 spent (4%); 2/3 consecutive rejects)\n294\tUpdate `META_INSIGHTS.md` and `FRAMEWORK_IMPROVEMENTS.md` THIS iteration — there may be no next one to do it in.\n295\t\n296\t## Cross-iteration files in THIS working dir (clean ownership — read all)\n297\t- `LEDGER.md` — FACTS (framework, read-only): every iteration's outcome + the tasks it MEASURABLY broke/fixed, plus the ones whose move was under 2·SE and so resolves nothing. Never re-introduce a change that broke a task; never redesign an edit because a task landed in `unresolved`.\n298\t- `JOURNAL.md` — HANDOVER (yours, append-only across the whole run): read the whole thing, then APPEND your entry for this iteration below the marker line. Do NOT edit earlier entries. This is how you avoid repeating refuted ideas and hitting the same plateau.\n299\t- `PROCESS.md` — EXPLAINABILITY (yours, REQUIRED this iteration): fill it in as you work (ranked issues, every edit + class, verify-the-fix, subagents/features used, what to preserve, what you skipped). It is snapshotted with your candidate.\n300\t- `RUNMAP.md` + `./prior_iterations/<id>/` — every prior iteration's PROCESS.md + capability diff, copied in for you. Read the ones targeting your cluster BEFORE proposing, so you build on prior work instead of repeating it.\n301\t- `INSIGHTS.md` — SUMMARIZED, VERIFIED findings (yours, append-only, optional most iterations): a distilled 'what worked / what didn't / what's promising' a future iteration reads INSTEAD of the whole journal. Add an entry whenever you have a new CONFIRMED finding (cite the RESULT that confirmed it).\n302\t- `META_INSIGHTS.md` — about the SEARCH PROCESS itself (yours, append-only): which strategies helped or stalled, what to try next. Update AT LEAST once, at the end of the run.\n303\t- `FRAMEWORK_IMPROVEMENTS.md` — cross-run suggestions for cap-evolve ITSELF, not this task (yours, append-only, optional): what confused you or was missing about the framework. Update AT LEAST once, at the end of the run.\n304\t- `.capevolve/run_epic-glm53-v5/rejected.jsonl` — ALREADY-REFUTED approaches (framework, read-only; JSON lines, one per rejected candidate with the gate's reason). Read it and make each proposal STRUCTURALLY different from what is in it. Its sibling `history.jsonl` is the same record for accepts.\n305\t\n306\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7da9b899-2570-4f45-9105-189f12061fa7","uuid":"40ca6871-d651-422e-a1df-1b8b03c1b978","timestamp":"2026-10-09T12:24:15.056Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic_case-016-component-canonicalization_0_0uz2244h/skills/epic-decompose/INSTRUCTIONS.md","content":"# Optimize the capability — ship several REAL, SAFE, VERIFIED fixes this iteration\n\n## THE READER (who consumes what you edit)\nAt runtime these capabilities are read by `rits/zai-org/glm-5-3` — capability tier: **strong**. The reader is a capable general model, but less robust than frontier on long or ambiguous context. Keep instructions clear and reasonably explicit; add a worked example for tricky formats; prefer code enforcement for behavioral rules over trusting inference.\nOptimize your edits for THIS reader's capability level, not for your own. When the reader is weaker than you, prefer explicit rules, worked examples, and code enforcement over terse prose you would personally infer.\n\n\nFocus: every failing task on the val split. Current val reward 0.969: 7 solid / 1 flaky / 0 failing of 8 tasks.\n\n\n\nGOAL: raise the eval score as much as you can THIS iteration, then STOP (the harness\nre-scores you — don't run evaluation yourself). The prompt AND the tools are equally\nfair game.\n\n## TIME-BOX (HARD — a prior iteration was killed at its turn cap for ignoring this)\nYou have a HARD turn budget (~200 assistant turns total, INCLUDING every subagent\ninvocation and every read of a rollout). A prior iteration of this run burned ALL of\nits turns on investigation and hit the cap BEFORE editing a single file — the harness\ndiscarded the whole candidate and the iteration was wasted. Budget yourself:\n- Roughly 25% of turns for diagnosis (read `./trajectories/` summaries + ONE full trace\n  per cluster, not every trace) — then STOP reading and start editing.\n- Roughly 50% of turns for writing the edits (the files themselves — the thing being\n  graded).\n- Roughly 25% for verification and handover (PROCESS.md + JOURNAL.md).\n- Do NOT run full benchmark cases / pipelines through the adapter to \"verify\" — the\n  harness re-scores the candidate for you after you STOP. Verification means checking\n  the edit against the trace it targets (the args, the gold, the violating step) — a\n  static check, not a full re-run. Subagent case runs are the single biggest turn-sink;\n  use at most ONE, only if a code edit cannot be checked statically at all.\n- If you are past half your turns and have NOT yet made your first file edit, STOP\n  investigating immediately and write the edits you already understand.\n\nMake **AS MANY real fixes as you can this iteration — solve many issues across many\ntrajectories, not just the biggest one.** A timid one- or two-edit iteration is an\nunder-used iteration: diagnose EVERY failure cluster in `./trajectories/` and ship a fix\nfor each one that passes the three tests below. Breadth is the goal — the more distinct\nfailing clusters you fix in this ONE candidate, the larger the gain.\n\nThe ONLY brake on breadth is regression: every edit must pass all three tests, because a\nsingle speculative edit that breaks a passing task can sink an iteration of good work (it\ndid in prior runs). So the discipline is \"many fixes, each one real and safe\" — NOT \"few\nfixes\". Do not stop at the first cluster; work through them all.\n\n## EDIT BOTH ARTIFACTS, AND PREFER CODE (this is where prior iterations fell short)\nA strong iteration changes BOTH the prompt AND the tool CODE — many edits to each. The\nrecurring failure mode to avoid: an iteration that just adds a few prose rules to the\npolicy and (at most) rewords a docstring, leaving `tools.py` logic untouched. That is an\nunder-used iteration even if it \"covers\" several clusters, because:\n- **A BEHAVIORAL miss is NOT fixed by a prose rule.** When the agent already \"knows\" the\n  rule but skips it (doesn't call get_*_details before acting, acts on a guess, re-asks\n  after consent, miscomputes a total) — adding ANOTHER prose rule it will skip the same\n  way does nothing. Enforce it in CODE: an in-body guard / validation / computation in\n  the EXISTING tool, or a composite tool that performs the whole action. Prose is reserved\n  for genuine KNOWLEDGE gaps (a fact/format/criterion the agent cannot derive).\n- **Do NOT defer a capability-gap cluster** (\"would need a compute tool — next iteration\").\n  If a cluster needs a compute/validation/composite tool, BUILD it THIS iteration — that\n  is the highest-leverage edit available, not something to postpone.\nSo for each cluster, first ask \"can this be enforced in `tools.py` code?\" — if yes, do\nthat; only fall back to a prompt rule for a true knowledge gap. Expect your `tools.py`\ndiff to contain real CODE (guards, validations, new/changed tool bodies), not only\ndocstring text — across SEVERAL tools, not one.\n\n## The THREE TESTS every change must pass (this is the whole game)\nBefore you keep any edit, confirm all three. Drop any edit that fails even one.\n1. **REAL** — it targets a cluster that is FAILING in THIS iteration's `./trajectories/`\n   (reward 0, partial-credit, or communication/omission). Never edit for a hypothetical\n   problem, never touch a path only used by already-PASSING tasks.\n2. **SAFE (bounded blast radius)** — the real regression question is *behavioral*:\n   **would this edit change what the agent DOES on ANY currently-passing task?** Not\n   \"does a passing task call this tool\" — \"does the agent now take a different action,\n   or newly ACT where it correctly REFUSED / escalated\". Two blast-radius classes:\n   - **BOUNDED** — an in-body guard/computation that fires ONLY on the exact violating\n     condition. Only already-failing inputs hit it; passing tasks are untouched by\n     construction. This is the SAFE default — prefer it.\n   - **UNBOUNDED** — any edit to a GLOBAL decision/permission/refusal rule in the prompt\n     (loosening \"X may do Y\", broadening who may take an action, relaxing a\n     refuse-and-escalate rule). It changes behavior across the ENTIRE decision class,\n     including tasks where the stricter/original behavior was the gold answer. Allowed\n     ONLY if the new behavior is correct for EVERY task in that class AND you have read\n     the currently-passing tasks in the class and confirmed none relied on the old\n     behavior. Otherwise it is a guaranteed regression — encode the discriminating\n     CONDITION instead (see the DECISION / PERMISSION lever below).\n   Name the passing tasks in each edit's blast-radius class and state which class it is.\n   A regression wastes the whole candidate (the gate rejects a net-zero), not one task.\n3. **VERIFIED** — you have shown it actually fixes its target (see VERIFY-THE-FIX). An\n   edit you cannot verify is a guess — drop it.\n\nQuality AND breadth: ship every fix that passes the three tests — the more clusters you\ncover safely, the bigger the gain. The only edits to leave out are the speculative ones\n(an edit that fails a test), not real fixes you ran out of patience for. Don't re-add\nanything `LEDGER.md` / `JOURNAL.md` show was already tried and rejected.\n\n## Read these first (everything is in this working directory)\n- **`./guidance/<cap>/SKILL.md` for EACH selected capability — READ IT IN FULL before you\n  edit; it is the MENU of improvement TYPES available to you.** Each capability skill lists\n  the concrete kinds of change you can make (for a tools capability: in-body validation\n  guards, normalization, computation, composite/atomic-write tools, loop/workflow tools,\n  enriched return values + actionable errors, add/remove tools, sharpened descriptions;\n  for a prompt capability: role/contract, decision-rule narrowing, missing-rule, worked\n  example, consolidation). Do not invent change types from memory — take them from the\n  skill, and deliberately apply MULTIPLE DIFFERENT types this iteration (e.g. several\n  in-body guards + a compute tool + enriched errors + a couple of prompt rules), matching\n  each cluster to the strongest type the skill describes for it. (Tool code? also read\n  `./guidance/sources/` for the data models/types so your code is correct.)\n- `./guidance/diagnose/SKILL.md` — the failure-clustering method. Use it.\n- `./trajectories/` — the FULL traces of the current best candidate (the step you build\n  on). The `## (b) 1 FLAKY task(s) — pass sometimes; make the good behavior consistent (full traces in ./trajectories/):\n(reward is the mean over trials — the honest signal; the feedback line is from the LAST trial and may say 'passed' even when the mean is below 1.)\n- case-019-content-vs-code-split (reward 0.75): decomposition_quality 4.0/5 — SCORE: 4\n\n**HLR Coverage — solid.** All four HLRs map to epics with per-epic traceability sections and a complete matrix: HLR-1→E002, HLR-2→E003, HLR-3→E001, HLR-4→E004; nothing orphaned. Priority inheritance is handled correctly and explicitly justified: E001 (nominally P1 via HLR-3) is raised to P0 because it gates both P0 epics. E004 is docs-authoring and terminal \n` block below summarizes them with argument-level feedback — read\n  the actual traces for the clusters you'll fix, don't rely on the summary alone.\n- `./LEDGER.md` — FACTS (read-only): every prior iteration's outcome + the exact tasks it\n  broke/fixed. Your SAFE test starts here — never re-introduce a change that broke a task.\n- `./JOURNAL.md` — the accumulating handover. Each entry is the optimizer's INTENT, and\n  directly below it the framework stamps a **RESULT** line (objective: ACCEPTED/REJECTED ·\n  Δ · the exact tasks fixed/broke). The RESULT lines — not the intent — are the truth of\n  what worked: read them all before proposing. If the most recent RESULT is **REJECTED**,\n  its batch was reverted; read that entry's `./prior_iterations/<id>/diff.patch`, keep the\n  edits that did NOT appear in its `broke={...}`, and DROP or REDESIGN the ones that did —\n  do NOT resubmit the whole rejected batch, and do NOT abandon the cluster. APPEND your new\n  entry (intent only) below the marker; never edit earlier entries or re-try a refuted idea.\n- `./RUNMAP.md` + `./prior_iterations/<id>/` — EVERY prior iteration's (accepted AND\n  rejected) PROCESS.md + diff.patch. Read the one(s) that touched a cluster you're about to\n  work on, so you build on what worked and avoid repeating what regressed.\n- `./PROCESS.md` — your REQUIRED explainability file for THIS iteration (template inside).\n- `./guidance/optimizer/<name>.md` — your agent's subagent/parallelism features (optional).\n\n\n## Process (do this, then STOP)\n**Parallelism:** Your agent supports parallel subagents/worktrees (see ./guidance/optimizer/claude-code.md). FAN OUT to cover MANY clusters at once: one read-only subagent per trajectory-group to diagnose, then one edit-subagent per issue (each in its own worktree), then MERGE every edit into this ONE candidate with no conflicts. This is how a single iteration fixes many issues across many trajectories, not just the biggest one.\n1. Read your capability SKILL(s) + the diagnose method + the cross-iteration files\n   (LEDGER facts, JOURNAL handover, RUNMAP for clusters you'll touch).\n2. Diagnose THIS iteration's `./trajectories/` ONLY (not stale signatures). Cluster ALL\n   failures by shared root cause — total, partial-credit, AND communication/omission.\n   RANK clusters by LEVERAGE = (# failing tasks × trials × score recoverable), biggest\n   first — but plan to fix ALL of them this iteration, not only the top few.\n3. For EACH cluster, pick the strongest improvement TYPE from the capability SKILL(s)\n   (cross-check the FAILURE TYPE section next) and draft the edit. Across the iteration\n   use MULTIPLE different types from the skills, not the same one repeatedly. Run each\n   edit through the THREE TESTS; keep it only if it passes all three.\n4. Ship every passing edit together in this ONE candidate — cover as many clusters as you\n   can SAFELY (that is the win), and never include an edit that fails a test.\n5. Fill `PROCESS.md` and APPEND your entry to `JOURNAL.md`. STOP.\n\n## Choose the lever by FAILURE TYPE\nPick the strongest lever YOUR capability's edit space offers (see `./guidance/<cap>/`).\nThe levers below are written for a TOOLS capability; for a prompt-only capability\n(system-prompt / skill-package) use the structural-prose equivalent noted in each.\n\n- **RULE VIOLATION** — the agent breaks a rule/precondition/formula it could already\n  follow. **Default strong lever (tools): move the rule INTO THE CODE BODY of the\n  EXISTING tool that governs it** — an in-body validation / normalization / computation\n  that raises an ACTIONABLE error or returns the corrected value, scoped to fire ONLY on\n  the violating condition. This is the highest-yield, lowest-regression edit and is what\n  drove the best prior results — reach for it first. *Example:*\n  ```\n  def book(record_id, amount, payment_id):\n  +   methods = {m[\"id\"] for m in get_record(record_id)[\"payment_methods\"]}\n  +   if payment_id not in methods:                      # fires only on the violation\n  +       raise ValueError(f\"payment_id {payment_id!r} not on file; available={sorted(methods)}\")\n      return _backend.book(record_id, amount, payment_id)\n  ```\n  (Prompt capability: make the rule unmissable — a checklisted step / worked counterexample.)\n- **CAPABILITY GAP / ACTION STALL** — the agent has NO reliable way to do the thing (a\n  hard-ZERO cluster needing a real compute / composite / discriminating-predicate tool),\n  or it narrates/confirms a multi-step action then never executes it. **Prose does\n  NOTHING for a hard zero** — a 0.00 task stays 0.00 after any docstring/prompt reword;\n  it needs a TOOL the agent will CALL that changes the graded state. (tools) ADD a NEW\n  code-bearing tool that closes the gap — a composite atomic-WRITE tool whose body\n  performs the whole action via the existing primitives (then REMOVE the raw primitives\n  so it can't be skipped), or a loop/validation tool. Add a new tool ONLY when it closes\n  a real gap AND the agent will call it AND it changes the graded outcome — NOT a\n  read/compute/summary helper that a guard or a prompt line would subsume, and never to\n  hit a quota. (Prompt capability: an explicit, ordered, unavoidable procedure.)\n- **KNOWLEDGE GAP** — a format/criterion/fact the agent genuinely cannot derive →\n  prose: a precise prompt or docstring rule. Don't restate a rule the agent already has;\n  that's a rule-violation (use code), not a knowledge gap.\n- **DECISION / PERMISSION (ACT vs REFUSE)** — the agent made the wrong call on a\n  decision the policy governs: it ACTED where it should have refused/escalated, or\n  refused where it should have acted. **This is the most dangerous cluster to fix\n  wrong.** NEVER loosen, broaden, or alter a GLOBAL decision/permission/refusal rule in\n  the prompt to fix it — a global prose change (e.g. \"restricted records MAY now be\n  modified\") flips behavior for the WHOLE class and regresses every currently-passing task where\n  the original, stricter behavior was the gold answer (this exact mistake sank a prior\n  run). Instead encode the EXACT discriminating CONDITION that separates the qualifying\n  cases, **ideally in CODE** — an in-body guard on the tool that owns the action, which\n  refuses/raises ONLY when the precise policy predicate is/isn't met (bounded blast\n  radius: only the qualifying cases change). If it truly cannot be code, add an ADDITIVE\n  prompt rule that NARROWS (states the exact predicate) — never one that LOOSENS.\n\nAlso improve **tool RETURN values / error messages** (actionable: what's wrong + valid\noptions + next step) when a recoverable error stranded the agent — this is high-leverage\nand low-risk. Never delete a needed rule; change/consolidate instead.\n\n## VERIFY-THE-FIX (do this for EACH kept edit — it satisfies the VERIFIED + SAFE tests)\n- **In-body guard / computation:** run the tool body on the EXACT args from the failing\n  trace and confirm it fires / returns the corrected value. THEN run it on the args from\n  1–2 currently-PASSING tasks that use the same tool and confirm it does NOT fire (the\n  blast-radius / SAFE check). A guard that never fires on the failing task is dead code;\n  one that fires on a passing task is a regression — drop or rescope either.\n- **Decision / permission edit (prompt OR guard):** the SAFE check is BEHAVIORAL, not\n  argument-level. Enumerate the currently-passing tasks in the SAME decision class (same\n  permission/refusal rule) and confirm the edit would NOT flip the agent's action on any\n  of them — in particular that it does not make the agent newly ACT where a passing\n  task's gold answer was to refuse/escalate. If you cannot enumerate and check that\n  class, the edit is UNBOUNDED and unverified — replace it with a coded\n  discriminating-condition guard scoped to the violating cases only.\n- **New tool:** construct the inputs the agent SHOULD have passed (from the trace's\n  observed state) and run the tool body; confirm it completes the action end-to-end.\n- **Prompt/docstring:** confirm the missing fact is now stated, general, and unambiguous.\n\nRecord one line per edit in PROCESS.md, e.g.\n`trace <task> arg x=<bad> → guard raises \"<msg>\"; passing tasks <ids> → guard does NOT fire`.\nAn edit with no verification line is unverified — verify it or drop it.\n\n## NON-OVERFITTING (every edit must GENERALIZE)\nEvery edit encodes a GENERAL rule that holds across the whole class of inputs — NEVER a\nliteral that special-cases one task (its id, target, name, or expected answer). A guard\nfires on the general condition (\"the id is not in the user's records\", \"the record is in\na state that forbids this action\"), NOT `if record_id == \"<TASK_SPECIFIC_ID>\"`. ALLOWED: constants the policy/domain\ndefines (the policy's stated current date, a fixed fee/threshold, a domain enum). Use\nper-task specifics and any ground-truth in the traces ONLY to understand the failure\nCLASS, then write the general fix.\n\n## Handover (REQUIRED before you STOP)\n- **PROCESS.md** (this iteration): the ranked cluster list (with leverage + RULE/GAP/\n  KNOWLEDGE tag), every kept edit + its lever, the VERIFY-THE-FIX + blast-radius line per\n  edit, what you deliberately skipped and why, and (if you used subagents) that you did.\n- **JOURNAL.md** (append ONE entry below the marker; never edit earlier entries). Write\n  INTENT only — you cannot know your gate result; the framework stamps the RESULT below\n  your entry: the changes I made (1 line/edit, naming the file/tool + cluster) · the\n  EXPECTED effect + why each is safe · which prior RESULTS I built on and which regressing\n  edits I did NOT re-try (cite ids) · refuted hypotheses (a prior RESULT disproved — never\n  re-test) · high-value clusters not yet cracked + designs already tried · plateau signal +\n  which lever to switch to · focus next iteration.\n\n## (b) 1 FLAKY task(s) — pass sometimes; make the good behavior consistent (full traces in ./trajectories/):\n(reward is the mean over trials — the honest signal; the feedback line is from the LAST trial and may say 'passed' even when the mean is below 1.)\n- case-019-content-vs-code-split (reward 0.75): decomposition_quality 4.0/5 — SCORE: 4\n\n**HLR Coverage — solid.** All four HLRs map to epics with per-epic traceability sections and a complete matrix: HLR-1→E002, HLR-2→E003, HLR-3→E001, HLR-4→E004; nothing orphaned. Priority inheritance is handled correctly and explicitly justified: E001 (nominally P1 via HLR-3) is raised to P0 because it gates both P0 epics. E004 is docs-authoring and terminal \n\n## Currently PASSING (7 task(s)) — your edit MUST NOT regress these\nThese tasks already score ~1.0. Preserve their behavior: any edit that changes their trajectory is a regression and will be rejected. Protect: case-013-human-process-gates, case-014-tech-preview-foundation, case-015-redundant-hlrs, case-016-component-canonicalization, case-017-near-threshold-no-triage, case-018-cross-strategy-dependency, case-020-hardware-dependent-investigation\n\n## What you are editing (the allowed edit space)\nThe capability under optimization is composed of these editable artifact(s). Use the FULL edit space below — do not limit yourself to trivial wording tweaks.\n- **skill-package** — Optimize a WHOLE Agent Skill package — SKILL.md, references, and bundled scripts — under the skill-creator authoring rules.\n  - Allowed edits: Edit ANY part of the package: the SKILL.md frontmatter (the `description` decides whether the skill fires at all — the highest-leverage text), the body, `references/*.md`, and the bundled `scripts/`. HIGHEST-LEVERAGE EDIT: TURN A SKIPPED PROSE STEP INTO A BUNDLED SCRIPT the body invokes by command line — a step the agent runs can't be 'forgotten' the way a body rule can, and a script's source never enters the agent's context (only its output), so it is cheaper than the prose it replaces. Two patterns to prefer: (1) the traces show the agent re-deriving the same helper or doing a deterministic transform by hand — write it once into scripts/ and say EXECUTE it, don't read it; (2) a check the agent keeps skipping — make it a script that exits non-zero. The script must be real working code (never '...' or docstring-only) with a `--self-check` entry point, because validation RUNS it before any rollout is paid for. Keep the body lean (<=500 lines) and push depth into one-level references with explicit pointers; the frontmatter/body/reference/script split is validated, so an invalid package is not scored at all.\n  - Full guidance (read it): ./guidance/skill-package/SKILL.md\n## How your edit is judged\nAlgorithm: hill-climb:all. Your edited candidate is re-scored on the SAME held-out val tasks and compared to the current best (val reward 0.969). It is ACCEPTED only if the per-task improvement clears a significance bar (a noise margin), so a tiny or lucky change is rejected. Aim for a real, generalizing gain across a CLASS of failures — not a one-off patch to a single task (that overfits and gets rejected or hurts the held-out test).\n\n## Self-check before STOP\n- You made your FIRST file edit well before half your turn budget ran out (a prior\n  iteration was discarded for investigating past its cap with zero edits shipped).\n  If you spent most of your turns reading traces, you did it wrong.\n- You did NOT re-run benchmark cases through the adapter to verify — verification was\n  static (trace + args + gold), and the harness's re-score is the real test.\n- Every kept edit passes the THREE TESTS (REAL, SAFE, VERIFIED) and has its\n  verify + blast-radius line in PROCESS.md. Drop any that doesn't.\n- You read each selected capability's `./guidance/<cap>/SKILL.md` and applied MULTIPLE\n  DIFFERENT improvement types it describes (not the same lever repeated).\n- You addressed EVERY failing cluster you found this iteration (not just the top few),\n  and did NOT defer a capability-gap cluster to \"next iteration\" — you built the tool now.\n- Your `tools.py` diff contains real CODE (in-body guards / validations / new or changed\n  tool bodies / a composite tool) across SEVERAL tools — NOT just docstring text. If the\n  only tools.py change is documentation, you under-used the iteration: convert the\n  behavioral / rule-violation / capability-gap clusters into code and try again.\n- Every BEHAVIORAL cluster (agent knows the rule but skips the action) is fixed in CODE,\n  not by adding another prose rule it will skip the same way.\n- You shipped many edits across BOTH policy.md AND tools.py — and you did NOT pad with\n  speculative/cosmetic edits or re-add anything already rejected (check LEDGER/JOURNAL).\n- For RULE-VIOLATION clusters on a tools capability, you used in-body guards on the\n  existing tools (the default strong lever), not loose prose.\n- For DECISION / PERMISSION clusters you did NOT loosen or alter a global decision/\n  permission/refusal rule; you encoded the discriminating CONDITION (in code where\n  possible) and confirmed it does not flip the action on any passing task in the class.\n- Every prompt edit is ADDITIVE knowledge the agent lacked (a fact/format/narrowing\n  predicate) — never a change to a decision the agent currently gets right.\n- For any hard-ZERO cluster you shipped a real tool the agent will call (prose does\n  nothing for a zero).\n- Any new tool closes a real gap, will be called, and changes the graded outcome — not a\n  helper a guard subsumes, not quota-filling.\n- No edit hardcodes a task-specific id/value/date/answer.\n- PROCESS.md + JOURNAL.md are filled. Keep narration minimal; don't restate these\n  instructions or explore unrelated files.\n\n\n## Run budget — THIS MAY BE YOUR LAST ITERATION (iteration 3/5 (2 after this one); $187.43/$5000.00 spent (4%); 2/3 consecutive rejects)\nUpdate `META_INSIGHTS.md` and `FRAMEWORK_IMPROVEMENTS.md` THIS iteration — there may be no next one to do it in.\n\n## Cross-iteration files in THIS working dir (clean ownership — read all)\n- `LEDGER.md` — FACTS (framework, read-only): every iteration's outcome + the tasks it MEASURABLY broke/fixed, plus the ones whose move was under 2·SE and so resolves nothing. Never re-introduce a change that broke a task; never redesign an edit because a task landed in `unresolved`.\n- `JOURNAL.md` — HANDOVER (yours, append-only across the whole run): read the whole thing, then APPEND your entry for this iteration below the marker line. Do NOT edit earlier entries. This is how you avoid repeating refuted ideas and hitting the same plateau.\n- `PROCESS.md` — EXPLAINABILITY (yours, REQUIRED this iteration): fill it in as you work (ranked issues, every edit + class, verify-the-fix, subagents/features used, what to preserve, what you skipped). It is snapshotted with your candidate.\n- `RUNMAP.md` + `./prior_iterations/<id>/` — every prior iteration's PROCESS.md + capability diff, copied in for you. Read the ones targeting your cluster BEFORE proposing, so you build on prior work instead of repeating it.\n- `INSIGHTS.md` — SUMMARIZED, VERIFIED findings (yours, append-only, optional most iterations): a distilled 'what worked / what didn't / what's promising' a future iteration reads INSTEAD of the whole journal. Add an entry whenever you have a new CONFIRMED finding (cite the RESULT that confirmed it).\n- `META_INSIGHTS.md` — about the SEARCH PROCESS itself (yours, append-only): which strategies helped or stalled, what to try next. Update AT LEAST once, at the end of the run.\n- `FRAMEWORK_IMPROVEMENTS.md` — cross-run suggestions for cap-evolve ITSELF, not this task (yours, append-only, optional): what confused you or was missing about the framework. Update AT LEAST once, at the end of the run.\n- `.capevolve/run_epic-glm53-v5/rejected.jsonl` — ALREADY-REFUTED approaches (framework, read-only; JSON lines, one per rejected candidate with the gate's reason). Read it and make each proposal STRUCTURALLY different from what is in it. Its sibling `history.jsonl` is the same record for accepts.\n\n","numLines":306,"startLine":1,"totalLines":306}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-92955a70d9c636ba","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
