ted per example (\"dependencies=\\\"{ID}-E002,{ID}-E003\\\"\"). OK.\n\nTitles with spaces — quote them. Values with quotes inside (triage_rationale) — not needed (no triage).\n\nLet me check tmp/ and then write. Also, let me confirm how frontmatter.py handles a file that has no frontmatter yet (body only) — the \"set\" on a fresh body file. The Step 0/8 instructions imply this works (write body, then set frontmatter). OK.\n\nLet me also quickly verify the epic-task file naming: RHAISTRAT-9016-E001.md etc. And signals files: RHAISTRAT-9016-E001-ai-signals.md. And summary: RHAISTRAT-9016-decomposition.md.\n\nThe batch-read command: `python3 scripts/frontmatter.py batch-read artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9016-*E[0-9][0-9][0-9].md` — hmm, this glob would match RHAISTRAT-9016-E001.md...E006.md. Wait, `*E[0-9][0-9][0-9].md` — E001.md matches (E + 001 + .md). Also matches -ai-signals.md? No — those end in -ai-signals.md. OK.\n\nHmm wait, one thing — does batch-read also match the decomposition file? RHAISTRAT-9016-decomposition.md — does it match `*E[0-9][0-9][0-9].md`? \"decomposition.md\" — needs literal E followed by 3 digits before .md. \"decomposition\" ends in \"...ion\" — no E+3digits. No match. Good.\n\nNow, before writing, let me reconsider the epic titles and content once more, and draft all bodies carefully. Also double-check: \"No cross-references to sibling epics... not in descriptions, scope, acceptance criteria, or HLR traceability.\" Careful in HLR Traceability sections — just list HLR IDs and text, no \"see E003\".\n\nE001 body draft:\n\n# Expose workbench last-activity data for console consumption\n\n## Description\nThe notebook controller already tracks per-workbench last-activity timestamps (used by the idle culler), but this data is tracked through Jupyter server API probes and is not available on a surface the web console can read — the console consumes the Kubernetes API only and uses no private component APIs. This epic makes the controller's existing last-activity data readable through the Kubernetes API so that per-project idle-workbench reporting can be built in the console without new data collection.\n\n## Scope\n- Expose the notebook controller's existing per-workbench last-activity tracking through the Kubernetes API surface (e.g., on the Notebook resource) — the exact surface is an implementation decision for the Workbenches team.\n  \n  Hmm wait — \"e.g., on the Notebook resource\" — is that inventing design? It's a hint. The constraint says don't invent \"CRD field names, or other implementation details not stated in the strategy\". Saying \"e.g., on the Notebook resource status\" suggests a mechanism. I'll drop the example and keep it functional: \"Make the per-workbench last-activity data that the notebook controller already collects readable through the Kubernetes API, in a form that per-project reporting can consume.\"\n  \n- Cover the data needed by per-project idle-workbench reporting: last activity per workbench. Hmm — also owner and reserved resources? Those come from the Notebook CR (owner annotation, resource spec) — already Kubernetes-readable. So E001 scope: last-activity only. The report epic reads owner/resources from existing CR fields. Hmm — but should E001's AC include \"data available per project for all workbenches\"? Yes.\n- Preserve existing culling behavior (the culler keeps working off the same activity data — no regression).\n- No changes to how activity is collected (probes stay as-is; only exposure changes).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Given a project with workbenches, the per-workbench last-activity data tracked by the notebook controller is readable through the Kubernetes API for every workbench in the project.\n- The exposed last-activity values are consistent with the notebook controller's activity tracking (what the idle culler acts on).\n- Existing idle-culling behavior is unaffected (culling continues to stop workbenches after the configured inactivity period).\n- The exposure introduces no private-API dependency for console consumers (Kubernetes API only).\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-1 (P1, partial — data source): provides the \"sourced from the notebook controller's activity tracking\" data for the per-project idle-workbench report.\n\nE002 body draft:\n\n# Expose stale pipeline-run metadata for console consumption\n\n## Description\nKubeflow Pipelines retains run completion metadata and artifact data, and pipeline runs are surfaced in the console today, but the console consumes the Kubernetes API only — per-run artifact-retention information needed to identify stale runs worth reclaiming is not established on a console-readable surface. This epic makes the stale-run data (completion time and retained-artifact information per run) available through the Kubernetes API so per-project stale-run reporting can be built in the console.\n\nHmm — wait. Let me reconsider. \"pipeline runs surfaced in the Dashboard\" — so run listing data IS available. The gap: retained-artifacts info. Let me phrase: \"Run completion data is already surfaced... the additional per-run retained-artifact information needed for stale-run reporting is not available on a console-readable Kubernetes surface.\"\n\n## Scope\n- Make per-run completion and retained-artifact information from Kubeflow Pipelines run metadata readable through the Kubernetes API, per project.\n- No changes to run execution, metadata retention policy, or artifact storage — read-only exposure of existing metadata. Hmm — \"retained artifacts\" — the report shows runs \"with retained artifacts\". The exposure surfaces which completed runs still retain artifacts.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Given a project with completed pipeline runs, per-run completion time and retained-artifact information is readable through the Kubernetes API.\n- The exposed data matches Kubeflow Pipelines run metadata.\n- Pipeline execution, run listing in the console, and artifact retention are unaffected (read-only exposure).\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-2 (P1, partial — data source): provides the KFP-run-metadata sourcing for the stale pipeline-run report.\n\nE003:\n\n# Per-project idle-workbench report in the web console\n\n## Description\nAdds an idle-workbench report to the RHOAI web console: per project, a report of workbenches that are sitting idle, showing last activity, owner, and reserved resources, so administrators can see reclaimable workbench capacity with confidence. Last-activity data comes from the notebook controller's activity tracking (once exposed on the Kubernetes API); owner and reserved resources come from the workbench resources the console already reads.\n\n## Scope\n- New per-project report view in the console listing idle workbenches with: last activity, owner, reserved resources.\n- Data sourced from the notebook controller's activity tracking for last activity, and from existing workbench resources for owner and reserved resources.\n- Report is read-only — no reclamation action (automated reclamation is out of scope for the strategy).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Given a project with workbenches, an administrator can open the idle-workbench report and see, per workbench: last activity, owner, and reserved resources (HLR-1).\n- Last-activity values in the report match the notebook controller's activity tracking.\n- The report is shown per project (project-scoped).\n- No reclamation or deletion action is offered from the report (out of scope).\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-1 (P1): the per-project idle-workbench report (last activity, owner, reserved resources), sourced from the notebook controller's activity tracking.\n\nE004:\n\n# Stale pipeline-run report in the web console\n\n## Description\nAdds a stale pipeline-run report to the RHOAI web console: per project, a report of completed pipeline runs older than a configurable age that still retain artifacts, so administrators can see reclaimable pipeline-run storage. Sourced from Kubeflow Pipelines run metadata.\n\n## Scope\n- New per-project report view listing completed pipeline runs older than a configurable age, with their retained artifacts.\n- Configurable staleness age threshold.\n- Data sourced from Kubeflow Pipelines run metadata.\n- Read-only — no deletion action.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Given a project with completed pipeline runs, an administrator can open the stale pipeline-run report and see completed runs older than the configured age with their retained artifacts (HLR-2).\n- The staleness age threshold is configurable.\n- Report contents match Kubeflow Pipelines run metadata.\n- Report is project-scoped.\n- No deletion action (out of scope).\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-2 (P1): the stale pipeline-run report (completed runs older than a configurable age with retained artifacts), sourced from KFP run metadata.\n\nE005:\n\n# CSV export for the idle-resource reports\n\n## Description\nAdministrators can export either console report — the per-project idle-workbench report and the stale pipeline-run report — as CSV, so report data can be shared and worked on outside the console.\n\n## Scope\n- CSV export capability for the idle-workbench report.\n- CSV export capability for the stale pipeline-run report.\n- Export available to administrators.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- An administrator can export the idle-workbench report as CSV (HLR-3).\n- An administrator can export the stale pipeline-run report as CSV (HLR-3).\n- Exported CSV content reflects the report as shown (same rows/columns).\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-3 (P2): administrators can export either report as CSV.\n\nE006:\n\n# Document the idle-resource reports and safe reclamation steps\n\n## Description\nDocumentation explaining the two console reports — the per-project idle-workbench report and the stale pipeline-run report — including what they show, where the data comes from, how to export them, and the safe manual reclamation steps an administrator follows to reclaim capacity. Reclamation in the docs is manual guidance only; automated reclamation is out of scope.\n\n## Scope\n- Doc topic(s) covering the idle-workbench report: contents (last activity, owner, reserved resources), data source, interpretation.\n- Doc topic(s) covering the stale pipeline-run report: contents (stale completed runs with retained artifacts), configurable age, data source, interpretation.\n- CSV export instructions.\n- Safe reclamation steps (manual actions to stop/remove idle workbenches and clean up stale runs/artifacts), with cautions.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Documentation explains both reports and their data (HLR-4).\n- Documentation explains safe reclamation steps (HLR-4), consistent with the strategy's out-of-scope boundary (no automated reclamation).\n- Documentation covers CSV export of the reports.\n- Documentation is technically reviewed against the implemented reports (reviewed by the implementing teams) — Rule 24.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-4 (P2): documentation explains the reports and safe reclamation steps.\n\nWait — \"reviewed by the implementing teams\" — is that a cross-reference to sibling epics? No, it's plain language. OK.\n\nHmm — E006 description mentions \"the two console reports\" — fine, plain terms.\n\nNow the summary body:\n\n# Decomposition: RHAISTRAT-9016 — Idle resource reporting in the web console\n\nSections: Overview?, Epic List, Component Mapping (canonical names), Dependency DAG, DAG Justification, HLR Traceability Matrix, Health Warnings, Tiered Delivery (note), (maybe Missing sections note inside health warnings).\n\nLet me think about whether to include an \"Overview\" — the required sections are the six listed. Extra sections allowed? \"Write the body content... with these sections:\" — the list is the minimum (\"minimum — add additional sections when...\" is for epic files; for the summary it says \"with these sections\"). I'll include the Component Mapping section since Step 2 REQUIRES logging non-obvious mapping