{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-0b950863-4c6c-4691-96b2-17efde36bccd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-8651368bb1182464","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic_case-016-component-canonicalization_0_0uz2244h/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9016-decomposition.md","content":"# Decomposition: RHAISTRAT-9016 — Idle resource reporting in the web console\n\nStrategy status: Refinement Done. Strategy priority: Normal. The strategy was not\ntriaged in Step 0: it spans multiple components and teams (console UI work plus\ndata-exposure work in two backing components plus documentation), so it is not\nbelow-threshold, and it requires code changes, so it is not docs-only.\n\n## Component Mapping (canonical names)\n\nStrategy component names were mapped to the canonical RHAI Jira component list\nin `.context/rhai-components.txt`. Non-obvious mappings:\n\n| Strategy name | Canonical component | Owning team | Basis |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| RHOAI web console | Dashboard | RHOAI Dashboard | Canonical list; `dashboard.md` (\"Jira component: Dashboard\") |\n| Notebook controller | Notebooks | Workbenches | The notebook-controller is the controller inside the Notebooks component (`notebooks.md`) |\n| Kubeflow Pipelines | Data Science Pipelines | RHOAI Pipelines | KFP is delivered in the platform as Data Science Pipelines (`data-science-pipelines.md`) |\n| Documentation (HLR-4 work) | Documentation | RHOAI Documentation (inferred) | Canonical list; no architecture-context file provisioned for this component |\n\nAll three affected components exist in the platform (architecture-context files\npresent) and are Konflux-onboarded, so no onboarding chain was generated.\n\n## Epic List\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Component | Team | Priority |\n|----|-------|------|-----------|------|----------|\n| RHAISTRAT-9016-E001 | Expose workbench last-activity data for console consumption | Implementation | Notebooks | Workbenches | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9016-E002 | Expose stale pipeline-run metadata for console consumption | Implementation | Data Science Pipelines | RHOAI Pipelines | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9016-E003 | Per-project idle-workbench report in the web console | Implementation | Dashboard | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9016-E004 | Stale pipeline-run report in the web console | Implementation | Dashboard | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9016-E005 | CSV export for the idle-resource reports | Implementation | Dashboard | RHOAI Dashboard | P2 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9016-E006 | Document the idle-resource reports and safe reclamation steps | Implementation (docs-authoring) | Documentation | RHOAI Documentation | P2 |\n\nNo Investigation epics: the strategy declares \"Open Questions: None\" and has no\nrisks, assumptions, pending reviews, or conditional ADRs. The remaining\nuncertainty (which exact Kubernetes surface carries the exposed data) is an\nimplementation decision that does not change which epics exist — captured as\nacceptance criteria on the exposure epics.\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[\"E001 Notebooks: expose last-activity data\"] --> E003[\"E003 Dashboard: idle-workbench report\"]\n    E002[\"E002 DSP: expose stale-run metadata\"] --> E004[\"E004 Dashboard: stale-run report\"]\n    E003 --> E005[\"E005 Dashboard: CSV export\"]\n    E004 --> E005\n    E001 --> E006[\"E006 Documentation: reports + reclamation docs\"]\n    E002 --> E006\n    E003 --> E006\n    E004 --> E006\n    E005 --> E006\n```\n\nCritical path: E001 → E003 → E005 → E006 (length 4). The workbench chain\n(E001 → E003) and the pipeline chain (E002 → E004) are fully parallel.\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| E001 → E003 | Rule 12/13 (producer → consumer) | The idle-workbench report builds against the last-activity data the exposure epic puts on the Kubernetes API; the console cannot render last activity until that surface exists. |\n| E002 → E004 | Rule 12/13 (producer → consumer) | The stale-run report builds against the exposed run-completion and retained-artifact data from Kubeflow Pipelines run metadata. |\n| E003 → E005 | Rule 12 (consumer builds on framework) | CSV export extends the idle-workbench report view and its data. |\n| E004 → E005 | Rule 12 (consumer builds on framework) | CSV export extends the stale-run report view and its data. |\n| E001 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring blocked by all Implementations) | Documentation describes what was built. |\n| E002 → E006 | Rule 11 | Same. |\n| E003 → E006 | Rule 11 | Same. |\n| E004 → E006 | Rule 11 | Same. |\n| E005 → E006 | Rule 11 | Same. |\n| No edge E001 ∥ E002 | Rule 14 (different repos) | Different components, different midstream repos, no shared artifacts. |\n| No edge E003 ∥ E004 | Rule 15 (same repo, different areas) | Both are Dashboard report views but integrate different backend data paths (workbench activity vs. pipeline-run metadata); merge-conflict risk is coordination, not dependency. |\n| No konflux edges | Rule 10 / Rule 25 N/A | All touched components are already Konflux-onboarded per architecture context; no konflux-onboarding epic exists in any chain. |\n\nEpic boundary decisions:\n\n- **E001/E002 are separate from the console epics** (Rule 1: different\n  component and team) and from each other (different components/repos). The\n  strategy lists the notebook controller and Kubeflow Pipelines as affected\n  components, and architecture context shows real exposure work in each: the\n  console consumes the Kubernetes API only (\"no private component APIs\",\n  `dashboard.md`), while notebook last-activity is tracked through Jupyter\n  server API probes (`notebooks.md`) and per-run artifact-retention data is not\n  established on a console-readable surface — the data exists, but not on a\n  surface the console can read.\n- **E003 and E004 are separate epics** despite the same component and team:\n  they integrate different backend data paths, map to different HLRs, are\n  independently deliverable, and are gated by different upstream exposure\n  epics. Merging them would couple the delivery of the workbench report to the\n  pipelines exposure work and vice versa.\n- **E005 is a separate P2 epic**: HLR-3 (CSV export) is a distinct, deferrable\n  feature spanning both reports with independent user value; consolidating the\n  export mechanism in one epic avoids having both report epics build the same\n  export surface independently.\n- **E006 is the docs-authoring epic** for HLR-4 (substantive new\n  documentation: report explanations and safe reclamation steps), per the\n  work-product type boundary.\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Requirement (abridged) | Epics |\n|-----|----------|------------------------|-------|\n| HLR-1 | P1 | Per-project idle-workbench report (last activity, owner, reserved resources) sourced from notebook-controller activity tracking | E001 (data source), E003 (report) |\n| HLR-2 | P1 | Stale pipeline-run report (completed runs older than a configurable age with retained artifacts) sourced from KFP run metadata | E002 (data source), E004 (report) |\n| HLR-3 | P2 | Administrators can export either report as CSV | E005 |\n| HLR-4 | P2 | Documentation explains the reports and safe reclamation steps | E006 |\n\nAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements. Priority inheritance:\nE001 and E002 are P1 as prerequisites of P1 HLRs. E006's priority follows the\ndocs-authoring exception (strategy priority Normal → P2), matching HLR-4's P2.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): the strategy has no Impacted\n  Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Acceptance Criteria section, and no\n  Non-Functional Requirements. Decomposition proceeded from the HLRs and\n  architecture context. Owning teams were taken from the architecture-context\n  files; the Documentation owner (\"RHOAI Documentation\") is inferred — no\n  architecture-context file exists for the Documentation component.\n- **Strategy claim vs. architecture context**: the problem statement asserts\n  \"the data exists; it is just not surfaced anywhere\". Architecture context\n  substantiates the *collection* claim but not *console access*: the console\n  reads Kubernetes CRs only and uses no private component APIs\n  (`dashboard.md`), notebook last-activity is tracked via Jupyter server API\n  probes (`notebooks.md`), and per-run artifact-retention data is not\n  established as console-readable. This is why two data-exposure epics exist.\n  If an implementing team finds the data already accessible on a\n  console-readable surface, the corresponding exposure epic collapses to a\n  validation of that access path — its acceptance criteria are written to\n  allow that outcome.\n- **Out-of-scope consistency** (scope trap check, passed): automated\n  reclamation/deletion and cost/chargeback are out of scope. The reports are\n  read-only and the documentation epic covers *manual* safe reclamation steps\n  only; no AC implies automation. Reserved resources are reported as\n  quantities, not costs.\n- **No priority inversions detected**: the strategy has no P0 HLRs; both\n  reports and their data sources are P1; export and documentation are P2.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nNot applicable — there are no external dependencies with uncertain timing; all\ncomponents are in-platform and GA. The two report chains (workbench-idle and\nstale-runs) are independent parallel streams, each delivering partial value on\nits own.\n"}}],"model":"zai-org/GLM-5.3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":61717,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-82c55a3249bf38e3","agent_id":"a08704833be2ccb20","session_id":"7da9b899-2570-4f45-9105-189f12061fa7","uuid":"ba65dc24-7315-46ee-92aa-332016cbecd4","timestamp":"2026-10-09T12:37:59.053Z","subag