{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-88322b36f003b91a","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-9016\n3\tscore: 14\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues: []\n7\terror: null\n8\t---\n9\t## Review Summary\n10\t\n11\tScore: 14/14 — pass\n12\tRecommendation: accept\n13\t\n14\t## Criterion Details\n15\t\n16\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n17\t\n18\tAll four HLRs map to epics, verified in both the summary's traceability matrix and each\n19\tepic's HLR Traceability section: HLR-1 (P1) → E001 (data source) + E003 (report);\n20\tHLR-2 (P1) → E002 (data source) + E004 (report); HLR-3 (P2) → E005; HLR-4 (P2) → E006.\n21\tThe strategy has no P0 HLRs.\n22\t\n23\tPriority inheritance is correct: E001 and E002 are P1 as prerequisites of the P1\n24\treports they enable; E003/E004 are P1 (HLR-1/HLR-2); E005 is P2 (HLR-3). E006's P2\n25\tfollows the docs-authoring exception (strategy priority Normal → P2) and correctly does\n26\tnot push priority onto the implementations it depends on — not flagged, per the exception.\n27\t\n28\tNo priority collapse: both P2 HLRs (CSV export, documentation) are separate deferrable\n29\tepics rather than bundled into the P1 report epics, and each of E003/E004 maps to a\n30\tsingle P1 HLR. The split matches the priority-split rule (HLR-3 and HLR-4 are distinct\n31\tfeatures with independent user value).\n32\t\n33\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n34\t\n35\tDiagram ↔ frontmatter cross-check (both directions): all nine Mermaid edges\n36\t(E001→E003, E002→E004, E003→E005, E004→E005, and E001/E002/E003/E004/E005→E006) exactly\n37\tmatch the target epics' frontmatter `dependencies`. Arrows are drawn dependency →\n38\tdependent, matching the stated convention. No cycles, no dangling references (the\n39\tstructural consistency check also reports no findings).\n40\t\n41\tEvery edge is rule-justified: E001→E003 and E002→E004 are genuine build-against data\n42\tdependencies (the console report consumes the Kubernetes surface the exposure epic\n43\tcreates — a real artifact dependency, not a configuration reference); E003/E004→E005\n44\tfollow the framework→consumer rule (export builds on the report views); all five edges\n45\tinto E006 implement the docs-authoring rule (blocked by ALL Implementations), and are\n46\tcorrectly not treated as priority inheritance or unjustified serialization.\n47\t\n48\tParallelism is preserved: E001∥E002 (different repos/components) and E003∥E004 (same\n49\trepo, different areas and data paths) carry no edges, so the workbench chain and the\n50\tpipeline chain run fully in parallel. No konflux-onboarding, license-validation, or\n51\trepo-onboarding epics exist (all touched components are already Konflux-onboarded per\n52\tarchitecture context), so the mandated-parallel implementation-type pairings are\n53\tvacuously satisfied and no forbidden konflux→general coupling exists. No conditional\n54\tbranch files are used and no main-plan epic references a branch node.\n55\t\n56\tCritical path E001 → E003 → E005 → E006 (length 4) is appropriate for a multi-component\n57\tM-size strategy (expected 3-4).\n58\t\n59\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n60\t\n61\tComponent/team tuples produce separate epics: Notebooks/Workbenches (E001),\n62\tData Science Pipelines/RHOAI Pipelines (E002), Dashboard/RHOAI Dashboard (E003-E005),\n63\tDocumentation/RHOAI Documentation (E006). The same-component Dashboard splits are\n64\tjustified: E003/E004 integrate different backend data paths, map to different HLRs,\n65\tand are gated by different upstream exposure epics (merging them would couple the two\n66\tparallel chains); E005 is the deferrable P2 export feature, consolidated into one epic\n67\tso the export mechanism is not built twice.\n68\t\n69\tComponent canonicalization is correct — the focus of this case: all four assigned\n70\tcomponents (Dashboard, Notebooks, Data Science Pipelines, Documentation) are names from\n71\t`.context/rhai-components.txt`, and the non-obvious mappings (RHOAI web console →\n72\tDashboard, Notebook controller → Notebooks, Kubeflow Pipelines → Data Science\n73\tPipelines) are logged in the summary with basis and are consistent with the\n74\tarchitecture-context files (dashboard.md, notebooks.md, data-science-pipelines.md,\n75\tincluding their Jira-component and owning-team headers). Teams match the\n76\tarchitecture-context owning teams.\n77\t\n78\tNo scope duplication across siblings: E003 and E004 build distinct report views against\n79\tdistinct data paths; E005 extends the report views rather than rebuilding them; E006 is\n80\tpure documentation. No work-product type mixing (code vs. content authoring) — the\n81\tdocs-authoring epic is separate. No epic is oversized; each is a focused,\n82\tsingle-deliverable unit well within a sprint.\n83\t\n84\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n85\t\n86\tAll six epics are Implementation, and each produces a concrete artifact: an exposed\n87\tKubernetes data surface (E001, E002), a report view (E003, E004), a CSV export\n88\tcapability (E005), and documentation (E006).\n89\t\n90\tThe absence of Investigation epics is justified, not an omission: the strategy declares\n91\t\"Open Questions: None\" and contains no risks, assumptions, pending reviews, or\n92\tconditional ADRs. The one residual uncertainty — which Kubernetes surface carries the\n93\texposed data — does not change which epics exist or what they do, and is correctly\n94\thandled as acceptance criteria on the exposure epics (including explicit\n95\tcollapse-to-validation clauses) rather than as an Investigation. This matches the\n96\timplementation-detail vs. genuine-unknown distinction.\n97\t\n98\tNo `gated_by` or `gate_failure_impact` is set anywhere, and none is required: there are\n99\tno Investigation epics and no gates in the DAG.\n100\t\n101\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n102\t\n103\tCorrect signal set per type: all six epics are Implementation and carry the 9-signal\n104\t`ai_signals`; none carries `investigation_signals`. All six `-ai-signals.md` rationale\n105\tfiles exist, and every frontmatter value matches its rationale table entry (verified\n106\tsignal-by-signal for all six epics).\n107\t\n108\tSignal values are consistent with the rubric and the architecture context: E001\n109\t`pattern_precedent=1` (the idle culler already consumes the activity data in the same\n110\tcodebase, per notebooks.md); E001/E002 `architecture_claims=1` (activity tracking and\n111\trun-metadata retention claims substantiated by notebooks.md and\n112\tdata-science-pipelines.md); E006 `human_process_gates=-1` (required docs technical\n113\treview sign-off, consistent with that epic's review AC).\n114\t\n115\tBorderline calls are calibrated rather than contradictory, each with a rationale\n116\tmatching its value: E001/E002 `open_questions=0` (the exposure surface is an unresolved\n117\tdesign decision but not an approach-changing open question — the honest middle between\n118\tthe +1 and -1 conditions, consistent with the strategy's \"Open Questions: None\");\n119\tE002 `external_dependency=0` (work location undetermined, but no upstream contribution\n120\tor vendor coordination identified); E003/E004 `open_questions=1` (report fields and\n121\tsources fully enumerated by the HLRs). The calibration consistently tracks where the\n122\tunresolved decision lives.\n123\t\n124\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n125\t\n126\tEvery epic has testable acceptance criteria derived from the strategy's HLRs, several\n127\tnotably precise (E004: \"runs shown are exactly the completed runs past the age\n128\tthreshold that retain artifacts\"; E005: exported CSV has \"same rows and columns as the\n129\tcorresponding report view\"; E003 enumerates all three HLR-1 report fields).\n130\t\n131\tRule-mandated ACs: the docs-authoring epic (E006) carries the \"technically reviewed\n132\tagainst the implemented reports\" AC. The rollback/feature-flag rule does not apply —\n133\tno epic replaces an existing capability (these are new reports, and E001 explicitly\n134\tpreserves idle-culling behavior by AC). The build-pipeline-green rule does not apply —\n135\tno konflux-onboarding exists in any dependency chain.\n136\t\n137\tNo epic body references sibling epics by draft ID — all epic-ID mentions are confined\n138\tto frontmatter (`epic_id`, `dependencies`); bodies are self-contained plain-language\n139\tdescriptions. The out-of-scope boundary (no automated reclamation/deletion) is enforced\n140\tby explicit ACs on E003, E004, and E006 — good scope-trap handling.\n141\t\n142\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n143\t\n144\tAll declared strategy scope is covered: all four HLRs and all three affected components\n145\t(console → E003/E004/E005, notebook controller → E001, Kubeflow Pipelines → E002).\n146\tHLR-1's three report fields (last activity, owner, reserved resources), HLR-2's\n147\tconfigurable age threshold and retained artifacts, HLR-3's \"either report\" export, and\n148\tHLR-4's report explanations plus safe reclamation steps all appear in epic scope and\n149\tACs. Cross-epic consistency holds: E001's per-project coverage of every workbench\n150\tmatches E003's per-project report; E002's completed-run and artifact-retention data\n151\tmatches E004's report contents; E006 documents both reports, CSV export, and manual\n152\treclamation, with dependencies on all five implementation epics.\n153\t\n154\tThe strategy's \"the data exists; it is just not surfaced anywhere\" claim is validated\n155\tagainst architecture context rather than silently trusted, and the residual risk is\n156\thandled through the collapse-to-validation ACs on the exposure epics. The missing\n157\tstrategy sections (Impacted Teams, Dependencies, Acceptance Criteria, NFRs) are\n158\tsurfaced in the summary's Health Warnings — correct handling per the review contract,\n159\tnot an unresolved completeness issue. Two observations, neither a defect: the\n160\tDocumentation owning team is inferred (no architecture-context file for that\n161\tcomponent), which the health warning already discloses; and the problem statement's \"a\n162\tsingle report\" phrasing is superseded by HLR-1/HLR-2/HLR-3 (\"either report\"), which\n163\tunambiguously define two reports — the decomposition correctly follows the HLRs.\n164\t\n165\tNo conditional branches are declared and none are needed (no bounded outcomes). The\n166\tepic set covers the full declared scope with nothing dropped.\n167\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-981e2a3418b8ea89","agent_id":"ae047d218f4fd1775","session_id":"7da9b899-2570-4f45-9105-189f12061fa7","uuid":"cc745227-994d-48e9-a1f1-2ba2dd04fc25","timestamp":"2026-10-09T12:52:06.693Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-88322b36f003b91a","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-9016 decomposition"}
